{
  "proposal_nominal": {
    "wife": 770000,
    "husband": 770000,
    "check": "House equity 700,000 + cash 50,000 + Volvo 20,000 = 770,000. 401(k) 400,000 + brokerage 300,000 + cash 50,000 + Tacoma 20,000 = 770,000. Schedule total 1,540,000 reconciles (1,100,000 - 400,000 mortgage + 400 + 300 + 100 + 20 + 20 = 1,540,000). Nominal equality is arithmetically correct as stated.",
    "citations": ["columbia_equitable_distribution.md"]
  },
  "proposal_after_tax": {
    "wife": 613008,
    "husband": 658960,
    "assumptions": "Each side valued net of taxes embedded in the assets it receives, sale/current value held constant. WIFE: house equity 700,000; eventual sale at today's value with 6% selling costs (66,000); amount realized 1,034,000 - carryover basis 300,000 (26 U.S.C. sec.1041) = gain 734,000; sec.121 single exclusion 250,000 -> taxable 484,000; tax at 15% LTCG + 3.8% NIIT = 18.8% (NIIT assumed per engagement convention because sale-year MAGI, 72,000 wages + 484,000 gain, far exceeds 200,000) = 90,992; net house equity 543,008. Plus cash 50,000 + Volvo 20,000 = 613,008. HUSBAND: 401(k) 400,000 pre-tax, taxed on withdrawal at his stated marginal rate 24% (engagement assumption; sensitivity: 12% -> 352,000, 32% -> 272,000) = 304,000; brokerage 300,000 with gain 80,000 (basis 220,000) taxed 18.8% = 15,040, net 284,960 (gain would be realized on sale; MAGI 218,400 incl. expected bonus exceeds 200,000 so NIIT applies); plus cash 50,000 + Tacoma 20,000 = 658,960. GAP: husband is 45,952 better off after tax, about 6.0% of the estate, because wife's package carries 156,992 of selling costs + tax (22.4% of house equity) vs husband's 111,040 (15.4% of the assets he takes). If wife never sells the house the gain is deferred, not avoided; a future sale at a higher price increases her tax.",
    "citations": ["irc_1041_121_71.md", "tax_rates_assumed.md", "columbia_equitable_distribution.md"]
  },
  "undisclosed_income": {
    "found": true,
    "amount": 50400,
    "source": "Granite Peak HR letter dated 2026-03-02 (target bonus 45,000 for 2026, paid 50% September and 50% February 'on achievement of plan,' with employee currently at 112% of plan -> 45,000 x 1.12 = 50,400 expected 2026 bonus). Corroborated by 2026-09-15 pay stub: YTD gross 146,212 vs salary prorate 119,000 = 27,212 unexplained, consistent with the September installment of 22,500-25,200. The sworn affidavit (2026-08-30) states bonus 'discretionary; none expected for 2026,' contradicted by the employer letter written five months earlier. Floor 45,000 (target) if 112%-of-plan performance is not accepted; the February 2027 installment of the 2026 bonus (approx. 25,200) had not yet been paid at the affidavit date. assumption (flagged): work performed during marriage is treated as marital property, consistent with the parties' own schedule treating the 401(k), which 'accrued entirely during marriage,' as marital - the library does not define marital-vs-separate property.",
    "citations": ["columbia_equitable_distribution.md"]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "name": "A. Original structure plus equalization payment to Wife",
      "wife_after_tax": 635964,
      "husband_after_tax": 635996,
      "description": "House (equity 700,000) to Wife; Husband keeps 401(k) and brokerage; Husband concedes an additional 23,000 of cash/brokerage so post-tax outcomes equalize (total after-tax 1,271,968 / 2 = 635,984; Wife was short 22,976). husband_after_tax shown reduced by rounding of 23,000 vs exact 22,976. Preserves the client's goal of keeping the house for the children.",
      "instruments": ["Refinance of the 400,000 mortgage into Wife's name with pay-off of Husband's half of remaining liquid funds at closing", "Quitclaim deed to Wife incident to decree (no gain under sec.1041)", "Promissory note / staged payment if Husband's liquidity is short; alternatively Wife receives 23,000 from the 401(k) by QDRO (pre-tax; its after-tax value to Wife depends on her marginal ordinary rate, not stated in the library - unverified)"]
    },
    {
      "name": "B. Sell the residence now, before the decree, on a joint return",
      "wife_after_tax": 619484,
      "husband_after_tax": 619484,
      "description": "Sale while married with 500,000 sec.121 joint exclusion: gain 734,000 - 500,000 = 234,000 x 18.8% = 43,992 tax; net 590,008; each side 295,004 + half of the liquid assets after embedded tax (401(k) half 200,000 x 0.76 = 152,000; brokerage half 150,000 - 40,000 x 0.188 x ... shown net 142,480; cash 10,000; car 20,000) = 619,484 each. Joint exclusion saves 47,000 of tax (18.8% x the extra 250,000) versus Wife selling later as a single filer - but the children must move at separation and the parties must remain married and file jointly for the year of sale, contrary to the client's stated goal. Listed to quantify the trade-off, not recommended as drafted.",
      "instruments": ["Listing/interim sale agreement executed before entry of decree (sec.121 500,000 exclusion requires a joint return)", "No QDRO required absent any 401(k) transfer between the parties", "Joint 2026 federal return (filing status fixed by marital status on Dec 31)"]
    },
    {
      "name": "C. Unequal division on grounds of nondisclosure (Rule 16.2(e))",
      "wife_after_tax": null,
      "husband_after_tax": null,
      "description": "Not quantified: the statute (Col. R. Fam. P. 16.2(e) as set out in the library) authorizes reopening the judgment and sanctions for failure to disclose income 'including bonuses and expected compensation,' including an unequal division in favor of the non-disclosing party's opponent; the library states the remedy exists but gives no formula, so the amount of an enhanced award is unquantified here (unverified beyond the rule's text). Minimum concrete ask: add the September 2026 bonus installment already received (22,500 at target; 25,200 at 112% of plan) and the February 2027 installment (approx. 25,200) to the marital estate, and require accounting of the 27,212 YTD excess; demand the Rule 16.2(e) remedy for the affidavit's false statement.",
      "instruments": ["Motion to compel corrected financial affidavit and production of bonus plan documents", "Post-decree motion to reopen under Rule 16.2(e) if the false affidavit surfaces later"]
    }
  ],
  "maintenance_tax_treatment": " Husband's proposal to make the 2,000/month x 36 months 'deductible to Husband and taxable to Wife' is legally unavailable: for divorce or separation instruments executed after December 31, 2018 (all instruments executed now), alimony is neither deductible by the payor nor includible by the recipient (post-TCJA, per irc_1041_121_71.md), and child support is never deductible or includible. Consequence: Husband bears the full 72,000 with no offset - a fact his counsel's draft ignores and which supports asking whether 36 months is the right length; Wife receives the 72,000 tax-free, which is favorable to her and should not be traded away for a nominal 'gross-up.'",
  "citations_maintenance": ["irc_1041_121_71.md"],
  "dependents_and_filing_status": "If the decree is final on or before December 31, 2026, both parties file single or head of household for 2026 onward (marital status fixed at year-end; per filing_status_dependents.md). Wife is the custodial parent (approx. 70% of nights, above the 65% guideline line in columbia_child_support_guidelines.md): under IRC sec.152(e) the custodial parent claims the children unless she releases the claim on Form 8332 - Husband's 'alternating years' scheme requires Wife to sign a release each of his years, moving the child tax credit to him in those years. Head-of-household status, EITC, and the dependent-care credit cannot be transferred by Form 8332 (they stay with the custodial parent). Recommended position: Wife signs no Form 8332; if trade value is needed, release only the dependency/child tax credit by Form 8332 for specified years - never head-of-household or care credits (they do not transfer in any event). Whether Wife qualifies for head-of-household for 2026 requires that she pay more than half the cost of the home and a child lived with her more than half the year ( IRC sec.2(b)) - with the house transferring to her in 2026 and approx. 70% of nights, that is plausible but fact-dependent; unverified until the residence arrangement is set.",
  "citations_deps": ["filing_status_dependents.md", "columbia_child_support_guidelines.md"]
}

