{"content": "{
  "husband_gross_used": 218400,
  "wife_gross_used": 96000,
  "combined_monthly_net": 18876,
  "basic_obligation": 3050,
  "husband_share_monthly": 2042,
  "notes": "Recommended worksheet (Worksheet A, income shares). Husband gross = salary 168,000 + expected bonus 50,400 (HR letter 2026-03-02: target 45,000 at 112% of plan; guidelines include 'expected or recurring bonuses evidenced by employer documents') = 218,400. Wife gross = earned 72,000 + maintenance received 24,000 (2,000 x 12) = 96,000, because the worksheet adds maintenance to the recipient and subtracts it from the payor. Net = gross x 0.78 / 12: Husband (218,400 - 24,000) x 0.78 / 12 = 12,636; Wife 96,000 x 0.78 / 12 = 6,240; combined 18,876 -> two-child basic obligation 3,050 (bracket <= 20,000). Husband (non-residential, approx. 30% of nights, below the 35% shared-parenting threshold) pays his share: 3,050 x 12,636 / 18,876 = 2,042/month; Wife's own share 1,008 (residential parent pays hers via direct expenses). Sensitivity: bonus at target only (45,000) -> Husband gross 213,000, share 2,023; no maintenance credit and no bonus -> 2,135; with bonus but no maintenance adjustment -> 2,294; salary-only with maintenance -> 1,830. Husband's counsel's stated basis (salary 168,000 only) ignores the documented bonus and the maintenance line; the combined-total basic obligation happens to land in the same 3,050 bracket, so the dispute is over shares, not the table entry. Combined monthly net 18,876 is inside the guideline table (top published bracket <= 25,000); no extrapolation needed. All figures assume the maintenance term as proposed (2,000 x 36); if the decree changes maintenance, the maintenance line and shares shift accordingly.",
  "citations": ["columbia_child_support_guidelines.md"]
}
", "path": "/workspace/deliverable/child_support.json"}

{"newString": "- **Also watch liquidity:** the current proposal leaves you with 50,000 in cash and a 6.1%, 2,648/month obligation. That is thin — after the refinance you should keep a reserve. Ask for part of the brokerage in kind rather than cash equalization: the same 300,000 carries only an 80,000 gain, and if the equalization runs through *cash* the tax-neutral path is brokered funds, not his pre-tax 401(k) (money taken from the 401(k) by QDRO rolls into your IRA tax-deferred and is taxed at your rate on withdrawal — the library gives no rate for you, so its present value is unverified).
- **The joint-return alternative costs 47,000 less in tax but breaks your goal.** If the house were sold *now*, before the decree, while both of you own and use it and file jointly, the §121 exclusion is 500,000 → taxable gain 234,000 → tax 43,992 vs your 90,992. That saves 47,000 in total tax and equalizes both of you at ~619,484 (see `division.json`, Alternative B) — but the children change homes at separation and you cannot buy back their stability for 47,000. I do not recommend it given your priorities; I list it because it is the price tag of keeping the house, and it argues for compensation elsewhere (Alternative A: an additional ~23,000 of liquid assets to you equalizes the after-tax totals).

If you keep the house, the mechanics are: refinance into your name, Husband signs a quitclaim deed incident to the decree (no gain to either of you under §1041), and the brokerage/cash lines fund his share. You then must own and use it 2 of the last 5 years at sale for your 250,000 exclusion — holding it 3–6 years satisfies that comfortably.", "oldString": "- **Also watch liquidity:** the current proposal leaves you with 50,000 in cash and a 6.1%, 2,648/month obligation. That is thin. (a) With respect, at least ask for part of the brokerage instead of part of the cash equalization — he keeps the low-basis 401(k); (b) put the **February 2027 bonus installment** (approx. 25,200) on the table: it is compensation tied to married-earned work, and his affidavit hides even the September portion already in his hands (library does not define marital-vs-separate boundaries — our assumption, flagged, is that work performed during marriage is marital, consistent with the schedule itself treating his 401(k), which "accrued entirely during marriage," as marital).", "path": "/workspace/deliverable/advice.md"}

